This version replaces the 1024-entry table plus linear fallback with one direct lookup that covers every 16-bit message number.

- **Full coverage:** numbers ≥ 1024 (for example manufacturer-specific 0xFF00–0xFFFE) used to fall into a linear scan, which ran to the end for unknown numbers. They now take the same direct lookup as all other numbers.
- **Cheap initialization:** entries store `index + 1`, so 0 means "absent". The table is zero-initialized for free, and setup writes only the entries that exist. Setup never touches the whole 128 KB.
- **Same results:** iterating from the last message down to the first means the first match wins, as in the original scan.
- **Safety fallback:** if `MESGS` were too large to fit the table, the function keeps the original linear scan.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Lut
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        bool ok;
        Lut() : idx(), ok(MESGS < 65535)
        {
            if (!ok)
                return;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                idx[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)(i + 1);
        }
    };
    static const Lut lut;

    if (lut.ok)
    {
        unsigned i = lut.idx[num];
        return i ? &mesgs[i - 1] : NULL;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```